": \"D008\", \"symbol\": \"Scheme\", \"type\": \"family of time-step maps and finite trajectories\", \"definition\": \"For tau>0 and mean-zero f in L^2, define Ftau(f)=(1/6)P[(S_tau Dinv f)^2]-(1/6)S_tau P[(Dinv f)^2]. Define Htau(f)=(1/3)P[(S_tau Dinv f)*Dinv Ftau(f)]+(tau/9)(S_tau Dinv f)*P0(f^2)-(1/54)[S_(tau-s) Dinv[(S_s Dinv f)^3]]_(s=0)^(s=tau)-(1/(27*tau))[S_(tau-s) Dinv2[(S_(s-tau) Dinv2 Ftau(f))*(S_s Dinv f)]]_(s=0)^(s=tau). Brackets are upper endpoint minus lower endpoint and products are spatial products. Psi_tau(f)=S_tau f+Ftau(f)+Htau(f). For a positive integer L, tau=T/L and t_n=n*tau; U^0=u0, U^(n+1)=Psi_tau(U^n), 0<=n<L. This is the unmodified scheme with no frequency cutoff. Its elementary L^2 well-definedness and continuity are proved below.\", \"depends_on\": [\"D003\", \"D004\", \"D005\", \"D006\", \"D007\"], \"requires\": [], \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"tau\", \"type\": \"positive real step\"}, {\"symbol\": \"f\", \"type\": \"mean-zero L^2 input\"}, {\"symbol\": \"s\", \"type\": \"endpoint dummy time\"}, {\"symbol\": \"L\", \"type\": \"positive integer\"}, {\"symbol\": \"n\", \"type\": \"integer step index\"}, {\"symbol\": \"u0\", \"type\": \"initial datum\"}], \"objects\": [\"Sob\", \"S\", \"P0\", \"P\", \"Dinv\", \"T\", \"tau\", \"f\", \"s\", \"L\", \"n\", \"u0\"]}}, {\"pointer\": \"spec.json#/definitions/8\", \"record\": {\"id\": \"D009\", \"symbol\": \"Pcut\", \"type\": \"family of orthogonal Fourier projections\", \"definition\": \"For real K>=0, P_(<=K) has Fourier multiplier 1_(|k|<=K) and P_(>K)=identity-P_(<=K), with identity the identity operator. These projections are proof devices and are absent from Scheme.\", \"depends_on\": [\"D002\"], \"requires\": [], \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"K\", \"type\": \"nonnegative real threshold\"}, {\"symbol\": \"k\", \"type\": \"integer frequency\"}], \"objects\": [\"Fourier\", \"K\", \"k\"]}}, {\"pointer\": \"spec.json#/definitions/9\", \"record\": {\"id\": \"D010\", \"symbol\": \"eta\", \"type\": \"averaged covariance of two real phases\", \"definition\": \"For tau>0, M_tau g=tau^(-1) integral_0^tau g(s)ds and eta_tau(alpha,beta)=M_tau(exp(-i*s*(alpha+beta)))-M_tau(exp(-i*s*alpha))*M_tau(exp(-i*s*beta)).\", \"depends_on\": [], \"requires\": [], \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"tau\", \"type\": \"positive real step\"}, {\"symbol\": \"g\", \"type\": \"integrable scalar function\"}, {\"symbol\": \"alpha\", \"type\": \"real phase\"}, {\"symbol\": \"beta\", \"type\": \"real phase\"}, {\"symbol\": \"s\", \"type\": \"integration variable\"}], \"objects\": [\"tau\", \"g\", \"alpha\", \"beta\", \"s\"]}}, {\"pointer\": \"spec.json#/definitions/10\", \"record\": {\"id\": \"D011\", \"symbol\": \"Phase\", \"type\": \"integer-frequency phase functions\", \"definition\": \"For integers a,b,c and k=a+b+c, set p=a+b, alpha=3*a*b*p, beta=3*k*c*p, Phi=alpha+beta=k^3-a^3-b^3-c^3.\", \"depends_on\": [], \"requires\": [], \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"a\", \"type\": \"integer frequency\"}, {\"symbol\": \"b\", \"type\": \"integer frequency\"}, {\"symbol\": \"c\", \"type\": \"integer frequency\"}], \"objects\": [\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]}}, {\"pointer\": \"spec.json#/definitions/11\", \"record\": {\"id\": \"D012\", \"symbol\": \"Nthird\", \"type\": \"positive third-largest-frequency function\", \"definition\": \"For nonzero integers a,b,c,d with a+b+c+d=0, let N1>=N2>=N3>=N4 be the decreasing rearrangement of |a|,|b|,|c|,|d|, resolving ties in any order. Nthird(a,b,c,d)=N3. Thus N3 is the second smallest of all four magnitudes including the dual output.\", \"depends_on\": [], \"requires\": [], \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"a\", \"type\": \"nonzero integer\"}, {\"symbol\": \"b\", \"type\": \"nonzero integer\"}, {\"symbol\": \"c\", \"type\": \"nonzero integer\"}, {\"symbol\": \"d\", \"type\": \"nonzero integer\"}], \"objects\": [\"a\", \"b\", \"c\", \"d\"]}}, {\"pointer\": \"spec.json#/definitions/12\", \"record\": {\"id\": \"D013\", \"symbol\": \"R2\", \"type\": \"family of trilinear Fourier operators initially on mean-zero trigonometric polynomials\", \"definition\": \"For tau>0, real base time t_n and k!=0, hat R2_(t_n,tau)(f1,f2,f3)(k)=-(tau/(18*i*k))*sum_{a+b+c=k,a+b!=0}exp(-i*t_n*Phi)*eta_tau(alpha,beta)*hat f1(a)*hat f2(b)*hat f3(c), with zero coefficient at k=0; Phase gives alpha,beta,Phi. Write R2^n_tau for t_n=n*tau. Bounded extensions, when used, are proved below.\", \"depends_on\": [\"D002\", \"D010\", \"D011\"], \"requires\": [], \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"tau\", \"type\": \"positive real step\"}, {\"symbol\": \"t_n\", \"type\": \"real base time\"}, {\"symbol\": \"k\", \"type\": \"integer output\"}, {\"symbol\": \"f1\", \"type\": \"first polynomial input\"}, {\"symbol\": \"f2\", \"type\": \"second polynomial input\"}, {\"symbol\": \"f3\", \"type\": \"third polynomial input\"}, {\"symbol\": \"a\", \"type\": \"summed integer\"}, {\"symbol\": \"b\", \"type\": \"summed integer\"}, {\"symbol\": \"c\", \"type\": \"summed integer\"}], \"objects\": [\"Fourier\", \"eta\", \"Phase\", \"tau\", \"t_n\", \"k\", \"f1\", \"f2\", \"f3\", \"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]}}, {\"pointer\": \"spec.json#/definitions/13\", \"record\": {\"id\": \"D014\", \"symbol\": \"pstar\", \"type\": \"positive real exponent\", \"definition\": \"pstar=23/14.\", \"depends_on\": [], \"requires\": [], \"binders\": [], \"objects\": []}}, {\"pointer\": \"spec.json#/definitions/14\", \"record\": {\"id\": \"D015\", \"symbol\": \"B\", \"type\": \"time-dependent bilinear distribution-valued map\", \"definition\": \"B_t(f,g)=S_(-t) partial_x[(S_t f)(S_t g)]. Initially use smooth inputs; the negative Sobolev extension to L^2 inputs is proved below.\", \"depends_on\": [\"D004\", \"D007\"], \"requires\": [], \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"t\", \"type\": \"real time\"}, {\"symbol\": \"f\", \"type\": \"first input\"}, {\"symbol\": \"g\", \"type\": \"second input\"}], \"objects\": [\"S\", \"Dinv\", \"t\", \"f\", \"g\"]}}, {\"pointer\": \"spec.json#/definitions/15\", \"record\": {\"id\": \"D016\", \"symbol\": \"J\", \"type\": \"time-dependent bilinear map\", \"definition\": \"J_t(f,g)=S_(-t)P[(S_t Dinv f)(S_t Dinv g)].\", \"depends_on\": [\"D004\", \"D006\", \"D007\"], \"requires\": [], \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"t\", \"type\": \"real time\"}, {\"symbol\": \"f\", \"type\": \"first input\"}, {\"symbol\": \"g\", \"type\": \"second input\"}], \"objects\": [\"S\", \"P\", \"Dinv\", \"t\", \"f\", \"g\"]}}, {\"pointer\": \"spec.json#/definitions/16\", \"record\": {\"id\": \"D017\", \"symbol\": \"Ctri\", \"type\": \"interval-dependent trilinear integral\", \"definition\": \"For I=[a,b], C_I(f,g,h)=integral_a^b S_(-t)P{P[(S_t f(t))(S_t g(t))]*(S_t Dinv h(t))}dt. Constant inputs are identified with constant time paths. At smooth inputs this is an ordinary integral, and the source estimates supply its continuous extensions.\", \"depends_on\": [\"D004\", \"D006\", \"D007\"], \"requires\": [], \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"I\", \"type\": \"interval\"}, {\"symbol\": \"a\", \"type\": \"left endpoint\"}, {\"symbol\": \"b\", \"type\": \"right endpoint\"}, {\"symbol\": \"t\", \"type\": \"integration variable\"}, {\"symbol\": \"f\", \"type\": \"first path\"}, {\"symbol\": \"g\", \"type\": \"second path\"}, {\"symbol\": \"h\", \"type\": \"third path\"}], \"objects\": [\"S\", \"P\", \"Dinv\", \"I\", \"a\", \"b\", \"t\", \"f\", \"g\", \"h\"]}}, {\"pointer\": \"spec.json#/definitions/17\", \"record\": {\"id\": \"D018\", \"symbol\": \"X\", \"type\": \"norm on time paths with weak derivative\", \"definition\": \"For an interval I, ||f||_(X(I))=||f||_(L^infty(I;L^2))+||partial_t f||_(L^infty(I;H^(-pstar))). Use the continuous L^2 representative wherever supplied.\", \"depends_on\": [\"D003\", \"D014\"], \"requires\": [], \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"I\", \"type\": \"time interval\"}, {\"symbol\": \"f\", \"type\": \"time path\"}], \"objects\": [\"Sob\", \"pstar\", \"I\", \"f\"]}}, {\"pointer\": \"spec.json#/definitions/18\", \"record\": {\"id\": \"D019\", \"symbol\": \"Stage\", \"type\": \"family of bilinear integrated quadratic stages\", \"definition\": \"For a base time a>=0, step tau>0, t in [a,a+tau], and constant mean-zero inputs f,g, F_a(t;f,g)=(1/2)integral_a^t B_s(f,g)ds. Abbreviate F_a(t;f)=F_a(t;f,f). Its endpoint representation and extensions are proved below.\", \"depends_on\": [\"D015\"], \"requires\": [], \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"a\", \"type\": \"nonnegative base time\"}, {\"symbol\": \"tau\", \"type\": \"positive step\"}, {\"symbol\": \"t\", \"type\": \"stage time\"}, {\"symbol\": \"s\", \"type\": \"integration variable\"}, {\"symbol\": \"f\", \"type\": \"first input\"}, {\"symbol\": \"g\", \"type\": \"second input\"}], \"objects\": [\"B\", \"a\", \"tau\", \"t\", \"s\", \"f\", \"g\"]}}, {\"pointer\": \"spec.json#/definitions/19\", \"record\": {\"id\": \"D020\", \"symbol\": \"KdV\", \"type\": \"distributional evolution equation and smooth-approximation solution convention\", \"definition\": \"KdV is u_t+u_xxx=(1/2)partial_x(u^2), u(0)=u0, with real mean-zero data on Torus. An exact solution at regularity sigma>=0 means its unique real mean-zero C([0,T];H^sigma) distributional solution obtained as the limit of smooth-data solutions. This convention alone grants neither existence nor a bound; these are proved from the registered flow source. For an existing solution…5566 tokens truncated…e rules remain available; no new substantive theorem is added.\\n39: \\n40: An output must be understandable and callable without reading its producer's\\n41: argument. Include the actual equalities/inequalities, binder types, hypotheses,\\n42: parameter windows, constant dependencies, representatives and a.e./pointwise\\n43: meaning. Global defined notation can be expanded from the packet. A formula\\n44: number, theorem name, or paragraph location cannot replace mathematical content.\\n45: Source locators belong in provenance fields. New mathematical prose uses direct\\n46: input IDs and previously proved local F IDs, not inherited numbered references.\\n47: \\n48: ## Object closure\\n49: \\n50: For every mathematical object actually appearing in a statement, definition,\\n51: local declaration, or proof, locate one legitimate declaration:\\n52: \\n53: 1. A frozen parameter, explicit logical primitive, or global D definition.\\n54:    State its meaning, type/domain and parameter dependence. Global primitives\\n55:    cover only core typed syntax, not a dictionary of subject-specific objects.\\n56:    New subjects use the same parameter/definition/local declaration mechanism;\\n57:    symbol registration never licenses a theorem.\\n58: 2. A binder inside the current proposition's full statement. Its scope remains\\n59:    that statement. A variable bound in an input does not become a free object\\n60:    in the consuming proof; instantiate the statement explicitly.\\n61: 3. A local declaration before first use, recording its kind, type, exact\\n62:    declaration, introduction basis, and scope. An arbitrary variable requires\\n63:    legitimate quantifier introduction; a definition uses already available\\n64:    objects; a witness requires an input or an earlier local existence proof.\\n65: \\n66: A declaration cannot grant existence, uniqueness, invertibility, regularity,\\n67: or a desired property merely by naming an object. Those obligations require\\n68: proof. Any D guard needed to use the object must be a direct R input, not a\\n69: theorem silently recovered from ancestor closure. Record mutually compatible\\n70: choices and witness dependence. Temporary assumptions stay inside R and must\\n71: be correctly discharged. Branch witnesses, local facts and local notation\\n72: cannot escape their scopes. Export a quantified or conditional statement when\\n73: needed, rather than a free private name. Global names are reserved; dummy\\n74: variables may be reused in disjoint local scopes.\\n75: \\n76: ## Dependency closure\\n77: \\n78: Inventory actual factual uses from the prose back to their grounds, not just\\n79: from an input list forward to a generic `uses` list. Each substantive imported theorem, prior project lemma or original\\n80: assumption must be a direct input. Eligible routine mathematical facts are recorded as mathematical audit notes\\n81: with actual local grounds, without requiring a native implementation lookup. A definition supplies meaning, not a theorem. An R output is not its\\n82: own premise. Adding the desired formula as an unproved input is not a repair.\\n83: \\n84: Explicit local calculations and fully proved intermediate facts may remain\\n85: inside R. Give a reused local fact a full F statement and identify its earlier\\n86: derivation and open assumptions. No separate graph vertex is needed for every\\n87: algebraic equality. A nontrivial imported result cannot be called a local\\n88: calculation. Substantive reusable lemmas should become N/R pairs; any fact\\n89: reused by another R must be exported in a complete proposition statement.\\n90: \\n91: The controller owns `spec.logic.calculations` and the small core syntax table\\n92: (`nl0/scripts/foundations.py`). The clean author tool binds omitted metadata and\\n93: rejects extra author permissions or task-specific primitives. This fixed\\n94: language grants no MVT, FTC, Fubini, convergence, compactness or regularity\\n95: theorem. Such facts need legitimate explicit inputs, an actual local derivation or\\n96: eligible positively reviewed mathematical background; the syntax table alone\\n97: grants none of them.\\n98: An author-written permission claim, in any field or prose, has no authority.\\n99: Ordinary typed arithmetic notation is part of this fixed language: natural\\n100: numbers, integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers mean their standard\\n101: structures with usual numerals and operations. Finite calculations include\\n102: their zero/one laws, such as `a+0=a`, as well as explicit algebra/order\\n103: derivations. Do not demand a separate E/M for an operation token or an allowed\\n104: finite calculation. This does not grant calculus, topology or measure theorems.\\n105: Conversely, inspect an actual displayed local derivation before demanding a\\n106: duplicate E/M input for its conclusion. A named consequence proved by permitted\\n107: finite algebra/order steps is not an imported premise. If a local derivation\\n108: fails, locate its unsupported implication rather than its absent theorem name.\\n109: \\n110: Metadata accounts for the argument but never replaces it. Copying all inputs\\n111: into a single `uses` record, registering a symbol without declaring it in the\\n112: proof, or writing a PASS label cannot establish closure.\\n113: \\n114: ## Operational acceptance\\n115: \\n116: 1. Author using the node template. Reverse-check the actual prose for missing\\n117:    declarations, unexpanded references and unlisted facts before freezing.\\n118: 2. Generate a packet for each R. The v2 generator supplies no original proof,\\n119:    producer proofs, provenance file locators, extra ancestor statements or\\n120:    undeclared ambient H. Direct source contracts and required definitions are\\n121:    included. Packet hashes bind the mathematical input actually reviewed.\\n122: 3. Review all closure/inference checks, admitting positively checked routine\\n123:    background notes under the shared policy. Record concrete packet locations and reasons for every verdict.\\n124: 4. Perform a fresh local mathematical replay. The reviewer gets the skill instructions\\n125:    and that packet, without original proof,\\n126:    other R bodies, author review\\n127:    notes, old verdicts, or an answer key. Record the actual carrier, packet hash,\\n128:    prior exposure and mathematical material read. Skill instructions supply no\\n129:    extra mathematical premise. Do not falsely claim a same-context rereading\\n130:    is a fresh replay. This rule does not authorize starting a new external\\n131:    service or agent; if the required carrier has not been used, keep replay\\n132:    UNVERIFIED and report that limit.\\n133:    Prior exposure means known task/proof material supplied or read in earlier\\n134:    contexts; this is not a claim about unknown pretraining contents. Learned\\n135:    mathematical knowledge cannot replace a missing declared premise or proof.\\n136: 5. An ineligible missing declaration, missing statement content, unlisted substantive theorem or need\\n137:    to look outside the packet blocks acceptance. Record the defect; do not\\n138:    silently repair it while assigning PASS. Return substantive/structural defects to authoring and review the frozen\\n139:    revision; eligible background notes need no author rewrite. Routine completion does not require a\\n140:    separate user interruption for every omission. Distinguish a representation\\n141:    defect from a substantive mathematical gap under the existing gap policy.\\n142: \\n143: The code rejects explicit malformed references, inventories, scopes, guards\\n144: and receipts. It does not infer hidden hypotheses from property words in types,\\n145: or decide theorem applications by matching a list of mathematical names.\\n146: These semantic obligations apply equally to every subject and remain part of\\n147: author inspection and Audit A. An omitted inventory entry or unnamed\\n148: theorem hidden in prose still requires actual semantic review. Freshness and\\n149: truthful reviewer identity need execution evidence; record shape/hashes cannot\\n150: authenticate them. No natural-language audit is a Lean kernel certificate.\"}"}
{"i":10,"status":"fulfilled","value":"{\"path\": \"nl0/references/external-theorems.md\", \"total_lines\": 128, \"start\": 1, \"end\": 128, \"start_column\": 0, \"end_column\": 76, \"requested_end\": 250, \"has_more\": false, \"next_start\": null, \"next_column\": 0, \"next_end\": null, \"text\": \"1: # External-theorem review: required Audit A standard\\n2: \\n3: Apply this standard to every retained E and every actual use of E in an R.\\n4: An E keeps the full source theorem; a derived specialization or substantive\\n5: translation is an internal N. Use the data contract's audit version 2. These\\n6: are reviewer obligations: a filled record or matching hash cannot establish\\n7: that a publication or a mathematical assertion is genuine.\\n8: \\n9: ## 1. Document identity\\n10: \\n11: Obtain and read the actual primary document. Match author, title, source kind,\\n12: publication/release information and exact edition/version to its provenance.\\n13: Record a traceable original source, retrieval date, local document and hash.\\n14: A search snippet, model answer, secondary citation, or functioning DOI/URL\\n15: alone does not establish identity. Books, papers and reliable preprints can\\n16: qualify; a DOI and journal publication are not universal requirements. Record\\n17: the source category and grounds for treating it as an admissible source.\\n18: \\n19: For missing originals, follow [ordered source retrieval](source-retrieval.md):\\n20: arXiv, Google full text, Google DOI discovery and confirmed-DOI Sci-Hub lookup.\\n21: Use normal browser readback when needed; unresolved access and verification\\n22: failures remain recorded. Then use the [conditional-source\\n23: procedure](conditional-sources.md) for unavailable text. Unavailable checks remain UNVERIFIED. A complete explicit\\n24: secondary statement can support a disclosed conditional run, not an ordinary\\n25: source-verification PASS.\\n26: \\n27: ## 2. Occurrence of the result\\n28: \\n29: Locate the claimed result in that document and version. Record theorem,\\n30: lemma, proposition or corollary label and page/section/paragraph/equation as\\n31: available; unnumbered results require an equally reproducible locator. Retain\\n32: the relevant primary text or a precise readback record bound to the document.\\n33: If the cited document merely points elsewhere without actually stating the\\n34: claimed result, follow and correct the source. An explicitly stated theorem in\\n35: an authoritative exposition can be cited as such; record its proof attribution\\n36: without claiming to have audited the referenced proof recursively.\\n37: Distinguish proved assertions from conjectures, questions, heuristics and\\n38: numerical observations. Preserve every condition of a conditional result.\\n39: \\n40: ## 3. Statement fidelity\\n41: \\n42: Compare the original full statement with the E statement, explicitly covering\\n43: objects, domains, scalar fields, spaces, hypotheses, regularity, quantifier\\n44: order, parameter intervals/endpoints, degeneracies, constant dependencies and\\n45: the exact conclusion (including a.e./everywhere, topology, uniformity,\\n46: existence/uniqueness and local/global scope). Record the notation map.\\n47: Faithful mathematical meaning, not word-for-word copying, is required.\\n48: Alpha-renaming is allowed. Never turn forall-x-exists-C_x into exists-C-forall-x.\\n49: \\n50: ## 4. Complete context and definitions\\n51: \\n52: Read the surrounding standing assumptions, definitions and conventions that\\n53: govern the result, including referenced context needed to understand it.\\n54: Capture any omitted chapter-wide hypotheses and normalization of measures,\\n55: norms, transforms, signs or scales. Map the source objects to the global D\\n56: ledger. Do not hide a condition outside the proof graph. A substantive\\n57: conversion requires an N proof; a renamed symbol is not that proof.\\n58: \\n59: ## 5. Version status and source eligibility\\n60: \\n61: Match every locator and statement to the chosen version. Check available\\n62: primary version histories, corrections, errata or withdrawal information at\\n63: the recorded date and scope. Record relevant findings and their effect;\\n64: explain use of an older version. Absence of a discovered correction is not\\n65: a guarantee that none exists. Unresolved conflicts remain UNVERIFIED.\\n66: Never import the target, an unproved project lemma, or a circular dependency\\n67: as E. Previously proved project modules require an expanded graph or a\\n68: versioned proof handoff. Do not attribute a combination of sources and a\\n69: project bridge to a single published theorem. A conjecture-dependent theorem\\n70: must retain that condition, which must be supplied legitimately at application.\\n71: \\n72: ## 6. Every actual application\\n73: \\n74: Give each actual E application an inference-local A ID. The same E applied\\n75: twice with different bindings has two A records, even within the same R.\\n76: For each A inspect the parameter/object bindings, prove every source\\n77: hypothesis from declared inputs or displayed local derivations, check types,\\n78: definitions and parameter ranges, identify substantive bridge N nodes, and\\n79: verify the resulting conclusion supports R's output. Record the evidence for\\n80: each individual hypothesis. “The theorem applies” is not a mapping.\\n81: Do not add an unproved hypothesis to close an application. An E passed at its\\n82: source does not automatically pass at any application site.\\n83: \\n84: ## 7. Ownership of derived work\\n85: \\n86: Put substantive specialization, scaling, change of normalization/domain/space/\\n87: norm/representation, combinations of sources, and prerequisite lemmas in N.\\n88: Each N lists its actual E/M/H/earlier-N inputs and its argument. Do not enlarge\\n89: the quoted theorem. Explicit elementary calculations and logical instantiation\\n90: may remain inside R; justify them rather than imposing needless graph nodes.\\n91: \\n92: ## 8. Separate decisions and reproducible evidence\\n93: \\n94: For each E write five separate review records: `literature_identity`,\\n95: `result_location`, `statement_fidelity`, `context_completeness`, and\\n96: `version_and_eligibility`. Each contains verdict, concrete notes and hashed\\n97: evidence. The first three must never be merged into one blanket PASS.\\n98: For each R/A write separate binding, per-hypothesis and conclusion reviews,\\n99: the source ID and the proved bridge IDs. Record actual reviewer, carrier,\\n100: shared context and reference material read. Self-audit is not independent.\\n101: \\n102: Use PASS only after the check was performed with adequate evidence; FAIL for\\n103: an established mismatch/omission/invalid application; UNVERIFIED when necessary\\n104: evidence is missing. Inaccessible text does not establish nonexistence. An\\n105: established absence of the claimed result in the cited source is a FAIL.\\n106: Retain failed records and the exact reason, source location and affected nodes.\\n107: \\n108: ## 9. Acceptance, invalidation and trust boundary\\n109: \\n110: Every retained E check and every application must PASS before the graph can\\n111: receive DRAFT-AUDIT-PASSED. A load-bearing FAIL blocks acceptance. Source-verification UNVERIFIED can\\n112: support only the explicitly qualified continuation above; other unresolved\\n113: mathematical checks still block it. Ordinary whole-proof acceptance requires\\n114: every retained check to pass. Never replace a\\n115: missing verification with a historical audit or a Lean build. Changes to the\\n116: source/version, statement, definitions, bindings or condition proofs invalidate\\n117: affected reviews; bind the replacement whole receipt to new canonical hashes.\\n118: \\n119: An inaccessible source suspends its citation verification, not the rest of\\n120: the workflow: continue available checks and explicitly conditional reasoning,\\n121: then report the unfinished citation checks and the UNVERIFIED overall audit\\n122: outcome. Descendants can be proved only relative to the explicit E assumption; the\\n123: whole result cannot receive an unconditional pass based on that source, and source inaccessibility alone is not a finding of mathematical error.\\n124: \\n125: List all external dependencies in the final verdict. This audit is relative\\n126: to those identified theorems; it does not independently reprove their published\\n127: proofs or establish a Lean proof of E. Mechanical checks establish record\\n128: coverage and byte consistency, not authenticity or mathematical correctness.\"}"}
